  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nMid level ridge axis situated northwest to southeast across the \nregion will sag slowly south through tonight as it gradually begins \nto weaken. The wildfire smoke will continue to affect the region \ntoday, especially for areas along and north of the I-70 corridor.\nHowever, the HRRR smoke model does suggest the better areas of smoke\nmay sag a bit more south-southwest into parts of our south late this\nafternoon and into tonight. \n\nHi-res models are hinting at some spotty shower activity popping up \nacross central portions of our area late this afternoon. This is \nperhaps trying to pick up on a differential heating boundary along \nthe edge of the better smoke fields. Confidence is not terribly high\nin this but will go ahead and include some isolated pops for \nshowers/storms along and south of the I-70 corridor for later this \nafternoon into this evening. \n\nThe other concern for today will be temps. The smoke helped limit \ntemps a few degrees on Wednesday so have made some similar downward \ntrends to temperatures for today, with highs generally in the upper \n80s to lower 90s. Between this and some dewpoint mixing this \nafternoon, expect heat indices to generally max out in the 95-100 \ndegree range this afternoon so will hold off on any heat headlines \nfor today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \n\nThe mid level ridge will continue to break down on Friday as a weak \nmid level short wave moves east across the mid Ohio Valley. Weak \nsoutherly flow ahead of this feature will help pull some better \nmoisture northeastward into the area Friday into Friday night, with \nPWs pushing up in excess of 2 inches. Instabilities will also \nincrease from the southwest through the day with a decent \ninstability gradient setting up across our area. MLCapes will range \nfrom less than 500 J/KG across our northwest to around 2000 J/KG in \nour southwest. This will all lead to a developing chance of showers \nand thunderstorms through the day on Friday, with the highest pops \nacross our west/southwest Friday afternoon into Friday evening. \nGiven the high PWs, locally heavy rainfall/isolated flooding will be\npossible with the storms.\n\nA broader mid/upper level trough will pivot down through the region \nSaturday into Sunday. This will help push a cold front southeast \nthrough our area later Saturday afternoon into Saturday night, \nleading to another round of showers and thunderstorms. There is some \nslightly better deep layer shear associated with this, so depending \non the exact timing, a few stronger storms may be possible late \nSaturday afternoon into Saturday evening, with damaging wind the main\nthreat.",
